Federated Learning adoption has been hindered in part because of communication challenges. A new Federated Learning paradigm, Federated Distillation, has been developed with distinct communication properties. It employs ensemble distillation and transfers model outputs as soft labels to participating clients and a central server via a public data set. Unlike conventional Federated Learning, such as Federated Averaging, which increases communication complexity with model size, Federated Distillation reduces communication complexity by utilizing the size of the distillation data set. We analyzed the potential for communication efficiency in Federated Distillation and found an optimal strategy, combining selective participation and priority assignment to clients with high expected distillation gains relative to the noise in their local updates. This approach, which we call Convergence-Guided Federated Distillation, offers a way to balance communication efficiency and model performance. We tested Convergence-Guided Federated Distillation on image classification tasks and found it to be a viable option for real-world applications, providing a way to streamline communication while achieving comparable results. From our experiments, Convergence-Guided Federated Distillation showed a communication efficiency improvement of up to 120 times over Federated Averaging, while the accuracy of the model was only slightly reduced, losing approximately 0.38%. Additionally, we found that priority assignment to high-quality clients led to further improvements in efficiency, averaging a 1.65 times improvement in efficiency for models trained on NoisyNet and a 1.53 times improvement for models trained on synthetic data. Our code is publicly available for review. 

\textit{Our work offers communication efficient solutions for FL and Federated Distillation, 
which can facilitate the deployment of FL in practice.} 

\textit{We discovered that priority assignment and selective participation are the key 
components contributing to the great efficiency improvement, and they can be used 
in conjunction to achieve FL models with state-of-the-art performance and very 
low communication cost.} 

